answer=Low-tilt approximation is the paper’s way of making program sampling practical when the target distribution has very high tilt.

The excerpt says they “deﬁne an \(F' : E \to X\) such that uniform samples on \(E\) map to a distribution \(q(\cdot)\) that is guaranteed to have low tilt, but whose KL divergence from \(p(\cdot)\) is low.” Then “the discrepancy between the distributions \(p(\cdot)\) and \(q(\cdot)\) can be corrected through rejection sampling.”

More concretely, they introduce a cutoff parameter \(d\) that “parameter-izes \(q(\cdot)\).” The paper explains that \(d\) “acts as a threshold, or cut-off, for the description length of a program; the distribution \(q(\cdot)\) acts as though any program with description length exceeding \(d\) can be encoded using \(d\) bits.” Formally, the excerpt gives:

- \(q(x) \propto 2^{-|x|}\) if \(|x| \le d\)
- \(q(x) \propto 2^{-d}\) otherwise

So, in plain terms, low-tilt approximation flattens the distribution by capping how much probability advantage very short programs get, producing a more balanced distribution that is easier to sample from, while still staying close enough to the original target distribution to be corrected afterward.
