answer=The paper says that **low-tilt approximation** is done by introducing a parameter **d** that “parameterizes \(q(\cdot)\).” The idea is that **d acts as a threshold, or cut-off, for the description length of a program**, so that:

- if \(|x| \le d\), then \(q(x) \propto 2^{-|x|}\)
- otherwise, \(q(x) \propto 2^{-d}\)

In the paper’s words, **“the distribution \(q(\cdot)\) acts as though any program with description length exceeding \(d\) can be encoded using \(d\) bits.”**

So the purpose of low-tilt approximation is to **replace a highly tilted target distribution \(p(\cdot)\)** with a **similar distribution \(q(\cdot)\) that has low tilt**, making sampling easier. The paper also says this discrepancy can then be **“corrected through rejection sampling.”**
